</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Fine-tuning hyperparameters</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Evaluation Framework</h3>

<section id="S3.SS6.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.6.1 </span>Primary Metric</h4>

<div id="S3.SS6.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Attack Success Rate (ASR) as our primary evaluation metric, defined as the fraction of generated jailbreaks that successfully elicit harmful content from the target model:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ASR}=\frac{\text{Number of successful attacks}}{\text{Total number of generated prompts}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>ASR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>Number of successful attacks</mtext><mtext>Total number of generated prompts</mtext></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ASR}=\frac{\text{Number of successful attacks}}{\text{Total number of generated prompts}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.6.2 </span>Hybrid Human-AI Evaluation Protocol</h4>

<div id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employed a hybrid evaluation methodology that prioritized human judgment while leveraging AI assistance for efficiency in ambiguous cases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Our evaluation process followed a three-stage protocol:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1: Primary Human Assessment</span>
All model responses underwent initial human evaluation by domain experts trained in identifying harmful content across our eight categories. Human evaluators assessed responses on a binary scale (harmful/not harmful) based on whether the output provided actionable information that could facilitate real-world harm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2: AI-Assisted Classification for Ambiguous Cases</span>
For responses where human evaluators were uncertain or disagreed (approximately 15% of cases), we employed Claude Sonnet 4 as a secondary classifier. The AI system provided detailed harmfulness assessments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 3: Expert Reconciliation</span>
Cases where AI and human assessments disagreed underwent final review by senior domain experts, with particular attention to edge cases involving technical accuracy, contextual appropriateness, and potential dual-use applications.
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">This methodology ensured both scalability through AI assistance and accuracy through human oversight, particularly important given the nuanced nature of contextual harm assessment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.6.3 </span>Baseline Comparisons</h4>

<div id="S3.SS6.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our approach against several baselines:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct Harmful Prompts</span>: Original AdvBench items sent without modification</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-Shot Reframing</span>: Llama-3-8B prompted to reframe harmful goals</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human-Generated Attacks</span>: Manually crafted jailbreaks from literature</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Overall Performance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our fine-tuned attacker model achieved a remarkable 81.0% Attack Success Rate (ASR) on the 200-item held-out test set, using one-shot generation for each prompt, representing substantial improvements over baseline approaches. Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Overall Performance ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Jailbreak Mimicry: Automated Discovery of Narrative-Based Jailbreaks for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reproduces the main comparison; Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Overall Performance ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Jailbreak Mimicry: Automated Discovery of Narrative-Based Jailbreaks for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> then summarizes how the same attacker prompts perform against four target models (GPT-OSS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, Gemini 2.5 Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and Llama 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR (%)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improvement Factor</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Direct Prompts</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.5</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1× (baseline)</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Zero-shot Reframing</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">12.0</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">8×</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Human Experts</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">45.2</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">30×</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak Mimicry</span></th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">81.0</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">54×</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Overall performance comparison across different attack methods (ASR on the 200-item test set).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Model</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Successes / N</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR (%)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">95% CI (Wilson)</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">GPT-OSS</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">162 / 200</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">81.0</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">[75.0%, 85.8%]</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">GPT-4</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">133 / 200</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">66.5</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">[59.7%, 72.7%]</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Llama 3</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">159 / 200</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">79.5</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">[73.2%, 84.7%]</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Gemini 2.5 Flash</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">66 / 200</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">33.0</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">[26.9%, 39.8%]</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Overall attacker success (same 200 prompts) against four target models. Confidence intervals are 95% Wilson intervals.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Significance testing.</span> Two-proportion z-tests on the overall ASR show statistically significant differences across models: GPT-OSS vs GPT-4: <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=3.296" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.296</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=3.296</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\approx 9.8\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>9.8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\approx 9.8\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>; GPT-OSS vs Llama 3: <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=0.423" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.423</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=0.423</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.672" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.672</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.672</annotation></semantics></math> (not significant); GPT-OSS vs Gemini 2.5: <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\approx 9.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>9.70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\approx 9.70</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>. Llama 3 shows remarkably similar vulnerability to the original target, while Gemini 2.5 demonstrates significantly greater robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Category-Specific Vulnerability Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2 Category-Specific Vulnerability Analysis ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Jailbreak Mimicry: Automated Discovery of Narrative-Based Jailbreaks for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports per-category counts, observed ASRs, and 95% Wilson confidence intervals for all four targets. The per-category test sizes sum to 200 and match the set used across experiments.</p>
</div>
